  "question": {
    "text": "On heating an aliphatic primary amine with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide, the organic compound formed is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "An alkanol",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "An alkanediol",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "An alkyl cyanide",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "An alkyl isocyanide",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reaction of&nbsp;aliphatic primary amine with chloroform&nbsp;and enhanolic&nbsp;potassium hydroxide&nbsp;leads to the&nbsp;formation&nbsp;of isocyanides/ carbylamines. This reaction is called carbylamine reaction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><math> <mrow> <mtext>R</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>NH</mtext> <mn>2</mn>&#160; </msub> <munderover> <mo>&#8594;</mo> <mtext>KOH(alc)</mtext> <msub> <mtext>CHCl</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> </munderover> <mtext>&#160;R&#160;-&#160;N</mtext> <mo>&#8801;</mo> <mtext>C</mtext> </mrow> </math><br /><br />This is the confirmatory test for primary amines. A foul smelling isocyanide is formed.<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>&#8800;</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>"
  }
}